WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.570 --> 00:00:03.130
The wilderness is an unforgiving place.

00:00:03.130 --> 00:00:07.770
There are so many different
ways to die out there.

00:00:07.770 --> 00:00:10.620
We've covered a lot of survival
tactics and situations.

00:00:10.620 --> 00:00:12.220
Like, a lot, a lot.

00:00:12.220 --> 00:00:15.310
But unless you know you're
gonna be attacked by a bear,

00:00:15.310 --> 00:00:16.810
you might have missed that one video

00:00:16.810 --> 00:00:19.500
about what to do when
you're attacked by a bear.

00:00:19.500 --> 00:00:22.613
That's why this video may save your life.

00:00:23.450 --> 00:00:25.270
OK, you're stranded in the wilderness.

00:00:25.270 --> 00:00:26.660
Congratulations!

00:00:26.660 --> 00:00:28.170
What do you do first?

00:00:28.170 --> 00:00:30.060
Whether in the woods, in the mountains,

00:00:30.060 --> 00:00:31.560
or on a deserted island,

00:00:31.560 --> 00:00:35.498
here are a few essential steps
survivalists say to follow.

00:00:35.498 --> 00:00:38.150
First, signal for rescue.

00:00:38.150 --> 00:00:41.050
The sooner you return to
civilization, the better.

00:00:41.050 --> 00:00:43.450
Your best chance of
escape is to be rescued,

00:00:43.450 --> 00:00:45.800
so use your resources to signal any ships

00:00:45.800 --> 00:00:48.330
or planes that might pass by.

00:00:48.330 --> 00:00:49.830
But if rescue doesn't come,

00:00:49.830 --> 00:00:52.940
the next step is to avoid injury.

00:00:52.940 --> 00:00:55.423
Your feet are some of your most important

00:00:55.423 --> 00:00:58.430
and vulnerable tools for survival.

00:00:58.430 --> 00:01:00.380
Boots will protect you
from cutting your feet

00:01:00.380 --> 00:01:04.010
as you explore the area for
the next step to survival.

00:01:04.010 --> 00:01:06.297
Find fresh water.

00:01:06.297 --> 00:01:09.491
While a human can survive
for 40 days without food,

00:01:09.491 --> 00:01:12.780
we can't live more than eight
to 10 days without water.

00:01:12.780 --> 00:01:15.040
If you have to choose between dehydration

00:01:15.040 --> 00:01:16.550
and unfiltered water,

00:01:16.550 --> 00:01:18.400
take your chances with the water.

00:01:18.400 --> 00:01:21.453
But if you can, fresh water
is gonna be your best bet.

00:01:22.570 --> 00:01:25.690
Sources of fresh water
include caves, rain,

00:01:25.690 --> 00:01:27.483
and making your own from salt water.

00:01:28.340 --> 00:01:32.380
If you're by the ocean, you can
convert salt water to fresh.

00:01:32.380 --> 00:01:34.200
Fill a can with salt water

00:01:34.200 --> 00:01:37.000
and then put a second
container over the can.

00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:38.270
This will collect the fresh water

00:01:38.270 --> 00:01:40.570
that evaporates out of the can.

00:01:40.570 --> 00:01:44.510
You need at least 1 liter
of water per day to survive.

00:01:44.510 --> 00:01:47.720
Look for signs of life if you
can't find a water source.

00:01:47.720 --> 00:01:50.010
Vegetation, birds, and insects

00:01:50.010 --> 00:01:52.770
can all mean nearby water sources.

00:01:52.770 --> 00:01:56.080
Roots, vegetables, and
cacti can all contain water,

00:01:56.080 --> 00:01:59.680
and mashing them with a rock
will release some liquid.

00:01:59.680 --> 00:02:02.138
Water flows downhill, so be sure to check

00:02:02.138 --> 00:02:04.788
low-terrain canyons and mountain bases

00:02:04.788 --> 00:02:07.030
that could be home to a water source.

00:02:07.030 --> 00:02:09.180
Morning dew can be collected with a cloth

00:02:09.180 --> 00:02:11.230
and then wrung out into your mouth.

00:02:11.230 --> 00:02:13.540
But just make sure you
collect it before sunrise

00:02:13.540 --> 00:02:16.400
or it could evaporate
before you get to it.

00:02:16.400 --> 00:02:18.410
If you're lucky enough
to catch a rainfall,

00:02:18.410 --> 00:02:21.660
use cups or any possible
containers to catch it.

00:02:21.660 --> 00:02:24.310
If possible, build a water-catching tarp.

00:02:24.310 --> 00:02:27.480
This will allow even more
water to be collected.

00:02:27.480 --> 00:02:29.950
So hopefully this never happens to you,

00:02:29.950 --> 00:02:33.210
but let's say you found yourself
in an extreme situation:

00:02:33.210 --> 00:02:35.330
You're out of food, you're out of water,

00:02:35.330 --> 00:02:38.850
and the only liquid nearby is your own.

00:02:38.850 --> 00:02:41.770
The Army has a field manual,
which has a do-not-drink list

00:02:41.770 --> 00:02:43.670
for just such an occasion.

00:02:43.670 --> 00:02:46.110
That list includes fish juices, blood,

00:02:46.110 --> 00:02:50.703
alcohol, seawater and, yes, urine.

00:02:50.703 --> 00:02:53.800
Drinking urine is basically
like drinking seawater.

00:02:53.800 --> 00:02:54.960
It's gonna dehydrate you,

00:02:54.960 --> 00:02:57.510
which is the exact
opposite of what you want.

00:02:57.510 --> 00:03:00.314
To make matters worse,
if you keep drinking it,

00:03:00.314 --> 00:03:02.150
the severe dehydration can trigger

00:03:02.150 --> 00:03:04.230
abnormally low blood pressure.

00:03:04.230 --> 00:03:07.450
Low blood pressure means less
blood flow to vital organs

00:03:07.450 --> 00:03:09.800
like your heart, lungs, and kidney,

00:03:09.800 --> 00:03:12.584
which can be damaged or fail as a result.

00:03:12.584 --> 00:03:14.750
That is not good.

00:03:14.750 --> 00:03:17.230
Adding to your trouble is urea.

00:03:17.230 --> 00:03:19.510
Urea is basically waste.

00:03:19.510 --> 00:03:21.550
It's not supposed to be in your body.

00:03:21.550 --> 00:03:23.570
That's why your kidneys
filter it out of your blood

00:03:23.570 --> 00:03:25.180
and into your urine,

00:03:25.180 --> 00:03:28.030
which ordinarily then
just leaves your body.

00:03:28.030 --> 00:03:29.970
But if you then drink the urine,

00:03:29.970 --> 00:03:31.910
your kidneys have to work twice as hard

00:03:31.910 --> 00:03:33.950
to filter out that extra urea,

00:03:33.950 --> 00:03:36.080
and that could lead to kidney failure.

00:03:36.080 --> 00:03:37.240
Seems like drinking urine

00:03:37.240 --> 00:03:40.060
might be your kidneys' worst nightmare.

00:03:40.060 --> 00:03:43.270
Just stick with fresh
water if you can get it.

00:03:43.270 --> 00:03:46.210
You should also know how to build a fire.

00:03:46.210 --> 00:03:49.640
You need a dry patch of
ground about 4 feet wide.

00:03:49.640 --> 00:03:52.400
Build a ring of rocks or
dig down a few inches.

00:03:52.400 --> 00:03:54.730
This will keep the fire insulated.

00:03:54.730 --> 00:03:58.960
Then use paper, dry grass,
or pine needles as tinder.

00:03:58.960 --> 00:04:01.650
Pile loosely in the center
and top with kindling.

00:04:01.650 --> 00:04:05.275
If that kindling's wet, you're
gonna need a lot more tinder.

00:04:05.275 --> 00:04:07.820
If you're lucky enough to
have a lighter or matches,

00:04:07.820 --> 00:04:10.850
ignite the tinder and
gently blow on the fire.

00:04:10.850 --> 00:04:13.130
If you don't have a lighter or matches,

00:04:13.130 --> 00:04:15.660
you need to use friction
to create an ember.

00:04:15.660 --> 00:04:18.760
You can also create sparks
with a flint and steel.

00:04:18.760 --> 00:04:20.910
If you need to, you
could always use a lens

00:04:20.910 --> 00:04:23.000
to harness the sun's light.

00:04:23.000 --> 00:04:25.970
As the kindling catches,
gradually add more.

00:04:25.970 --> 00:04:28.070
Once you have a nice, healthy flame going,

00:04:28.070 --> 00:04:30.020
begin to add the firewood.

00:04:30.020 --> 00:04:32.530
Arrange the pieces in a tepee shape.

00:04:32.530 --> 00:04:34.590
This fire will burn hot and quickly,

00:04:34.590 --> 00:04:36.053
so be sure to keep it fed.

00:04:37.010 --> 00:04:38.410
As you explore the area,

00:04:38.410 --> 00:04:41.674
you might be trying to find
your way back to civilization.

00:04:41.674 --> 00:04:43.972
You may remember hearing
how moss only grows

00:04:43.972 --> 00:04:45.763
on the north side of trees.

00:04:45.763 --> 00:04:46.938
Great, right?

00:04:46.938 --> 00:04:49.350
Unfortunately, that's a myth.

00:04:49.350 --> 00:04:52.940
Moss grows wherever
conditions are moist and cool.

00:04:52.940 --> 00:04:54.750
This is often the north side of trees

00:04:54.750 --> 00:04:57.360
since they are less likely
to receive direct sunlight,

00:04:57.360 --> 00:05:00.880
but in the woods, trees can
be shaded from any direction,

00:05:00.880 --> 00:05:03.891
so it's not safe to go by this rule.

00:05:03.891 --> 00:05:05.720
There are some unseen dangers

00:05:05.720 --> 00:05:07.690
that could definitely slow you down.

00:05:07.690 --> 00:05:10.707
You should also know
how to deal with these.

00:05:10.707 --> 00:05:13.897
Chances are you won't
encounter quicksand that often,

00:05:13.897 --> 00:05:17.750
but just in case you do,
here's how to escape quicksand.

00:05:17.750 --> 00:05:19.470
First of all, don't panic.

00:05:19.470 --> 00:05:21.400
It's impossible to drown in quicksand

00:05:21.400 --> 00:05:23.140
because humans actually float in it.

00:05:23.140 --> 00:05:26.340
What makes it so dangerous
is its viscosity.

00:05:26.340 --> 00:05:29.330
Once disturbed, quicksand
becomes much more viscous,

00:05:29.330 --> 00:05:31.560
trapping whatever it envelops.

00:05:31.560 --> 00:05:33.620
Don't ask your friend to pull you out;

00:05:33.620 --> 00:05:36.100
they'll only be able to
dislodge your top half,

00:05:36.100 --> 00:05:37.710
and that's no good.

00:05:37.710 --> 00:05:39.780
Here's how you get out of it.

00:05:39.780 --> 00:05:41.670
Wiggle your legs to create a pocket

00:05:41.670 --> 00:05:43.330
for water to trickle down.

00:05:43.330 --> 00:05:46.040
This will loosen the
sand around your legs.

00:05:46.040 --> 00:05:48.530
Feel free to lose your
shoes if you have to.

00:05:48.530 --> 00:05:50.570
Next, lay back.

00:05:50.570 --> 00:05:53.470
The more you distribute your
weight across the surface,

00:05:53.470 --> 00:05:55.900
the harder it will be to sink.

00:05:55.900 --> 00:05:58.180
Now work on your backstroke.

00:05:58.180 --> 00:06:00.890
Use your arms to propel
yourself backwards.

00:06:00.890 --> 00:06:03.210
The quicksand will
actually keep you afloat,

00:06:03.210 --> 00:06:05.670
and you'll be able to pull your legs free.

00:06:05.670 --> 00:06:09.300
Once you reach solid ground,
roll away from the quicksand,

00:06:09.300 --> 00:06:12.540
and then you'll be clear
of that sticky situation.

00:06:12.540 --> 00:06:15.575
Mother Nature isn't the
only danger out here.

00:06:15.575 --> 00:06:17.370
Wildlife is also a threat

00:06:17.370 --> 00:06:20.391
if you don't know how
to react appropriately.

00:06:20.391 --> 00:06:21.530
Like snakes.

00:06:21.530 --> 00:06:23.840
Most snakes will avoid you,

00:06:23.840 --> 00:06:26.430
but if you happen to get
bitten by venomous snakes,

00:06:26.430 --> 00:06:28.620
you can always suck the venom out, right?

00:06:28.620 --> 00:06:31.340
Nope, that is another myth.

00:06:31.340 --> 00:06:32.930
A venomous bite delivers the venom

00:06:32.930 --> 00:06:34.660
straight to your bloodstream.

00:06:34.660 --> 00:06:36.504
In fact, trying to suck it out

00:06:36.504 --> 00:06:38.872
could transfer the venom to your mouth.

00:06:38.872 --> 00:06:41.750
It could also further infect the wound.

00:06:41.750 --> 00:06:45.740
Instead, hold the bitten limb
below or close to heart level.

00:06:45.740 --> 00:06:49.118
This may prevent the venom
from reaching your heart.

00:06:49.118 --> 00:06:50.690
If you're in some parts of the world,

00:06:50.690 --> 00:06:54.390
you have a good chance of
running across some bears.

00:06:54.390 --> 00:06:55.872
Coyote Peterson: Do not take
out your phone and think,

00:06:55.872 --> 00:06:58.360
"Ooh, there's a bear, I'm
gonna get a photograph of it."

00:06:58.360 --> 00:07:00.612
You see a bear, that bear hasn't seen you,

00:07:00.612 --> 00:07:04.010
the best thing to do is
slowly backstep yourself

00:07:04.010 --> 00:07:05.385
out of the situation.

00:07:05.385 --> 00:07:07.880
If you do encounter a bear
and that bear does see you,

00:07:07.880 --> 00:07:10.593
more times than not, the bear
is actually more afraid of you

00:07:10.593 --> 00:07:11.510
than you should be of it,

00:07:11.510 --> 00:07:13.460
and it will take off on its own.

00:07:13.460 --> 00:07:16.420
If you are attacked, do
not try to outrun a bear.

00:07:16.420 --> 00:07:17.924
I know a lot of people out there who say,

00:07:17.924 --> 00:07:19.440
"I could outrun a bear."

00:07:19.440 --> 00:07:20.780
You can't outrun a bear.

00:07:20.780 --> 00:07:22.750
However if it makes a threat towards you,

00:07:22.750 --> 00:07:24.390
make yourself look big.

00:07:24.390 --> 00:07:27.600
Scream, act crazy, do whatever you can.

00:07:27.600 --> 00:07:30.005
Tim MacWelch: In the event
of an attack by a black bear,

00:07:30.005 --> 00:07:33.540
the most important thing you
can do is fight for your life.

00:07:33.540 --> 00:07:35.630
A grizzly bear, however, is much larger,

00:07:35.630 --> 00:07:37.970
much more powerful, and
way out of our league

00:07:37.970 --> 00:07:39.270
for a hand-to-hand fight.

00:07:39.270 --> 00:07:41.810
With a grizzly bear, your
best chance of survival

00:07:41.810 --> 00:07:45.530
is to curl up in a ball, play
dead, and cry for your mama.

00:07:45.530 --> 00:07:48.070
Peterson: Protect your head
and your neck and just hold on,

00:07:48.070 --> 00:07:50.170
because it's probably gonna
be a very painful ride.

00:07:50.170 --> 00:07:53.280
But oftentimes, that bear will
end up leaving and going on.

00:07:53.280 --> 00:07:55.650
Give yourself a few minutes,
endure through the pain,

00:07:55.650 --> 00:07:56.930
and once you know that the animal

00:07:56.930 --> 00:07:58.540
has moved off into the underbrush,

00:07:58.540 --> 00:08:00.150
that's when you quietly get yourself up

00:08:00.150 --> 00:08:03.609
and you go seek medical
attention as quick as possible.

00:08:03.609 --> 00:08:05.680
Narrator: Probably the
most important lesson

00:08:05.680 --> 00:08:08.060
to survive in an unfamiliar area

00:08:08.060 --> 00:08:09.920
is to become familiar with it.

00:08:09.920 --> 00:08:13.030
Study the terrain,
environment, and wildlife

00:08:13.030 --> 00:08:16.020
of any new area before you enter it.

00:08:16.020 --> 00:08:17.950
Let people know where you will be

00:08:17.950 --> 00:08:19.930
and how long you'll be out there.

00:08:19.930 --> 00:08:22.510
And bring the necessary gear for that trip

00:08:22.510 --> 00:08:25.990
and every emergency situation
that you can think of.

00:08:25.990 --> 00:08:28.580
Using these tips,
hopefully you'll never need

00:08:28.580 --> 00:08:30.903
to drink your own pee in the first place.

